  {
    "title": "Ratio spectra of polynomial-growth integer sequences",
    "description": "For the anti-Fibonacci sequence A(n) = 1 + n(n-1)/2 the consecutive ratio A(n+1)/A(n) decreases monotonically to 1. Conjecture: for every integer sequence of exact polynomial degree d ≥ 1 (i.e. B(n) = c·n^d + lower order with c > 0), the consecutive ratio B(n+1)/B(n) converges to 1, and the scaled deviation n·(B(n+1)/B(n) - 1) converges to the degree d. Proposed approach: expand B(n+1)/B(n) = (1 + 1/n)^d · (1 + o(1)) and extract the leading correction. This isolates 'degree' as an observable read off purely from ratio asymptotics, sharply separating polynomial sequences (ratio → 1) from geometric ones (ratio → constant > 1 such as the golden ratio).",
    "tags": ["SequenceAsymptotics", "NumberTheory"]
  },
  {
    "title": "A rationality dichotomy for limits of consecutive ratios",
    "description": "The anti-Fibonacci ratio converges to the rational number 1, while the Fibonacci ratio converges to the irrational golden ratio. Conjecture: if a sequence satisfies a linear recurrence with constant coefficients whose characteristic polynomial has a single dominant real root r, then the consecutive ratio converges to r, and r is rational if and only if the polynomial factors over the rationals with a rational dominant root. Approach: combine the theory of linear recurrences with irrationality criteria for algebraic integers. The dichotomy explains structurally why 'additive' recurrences (Fibonacci) land on irrationals while 'polynomial' sequences land on 1.",
    "tags": ["LinearRecurrences", "Irrationality"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Sharp sub-Fibonacci growth window",
    "description": "We proved A(n+2) < A(n+1) + A(n) for n ≥ 4, with equality at n = 3 and reversal for small n. Conjecture: for any quadratic sequence Q(n) = an^2 + bn + c with a > 0, the inequality Q(n+2) < Q(n+1) + Q(n) holds for all sufficiently large n, and the exact threshold n0 is given by the largest root of the quadratic a·n^2 - (3a - ...)·n + ... obtained by subtracting the two sides. Approach: reduce the three-term comparison to a single quadratic in n via the closed form and solve for the sign-change point. This turns 'anti-Fibonacci behavior' into an exactly computable crossover threshold.",
    "tags": ["Inequalities", "Combinatorics"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Density zero of the additive-sum complement",
    "description": "Call m an 'additive value' of A if m = A(k+1) + A(k) for some k. Since A grows like n^2/2, the additive values grow like n^2, so among the first N integers only O(sqrt N) are additive values. Conjecture: the set of additive values has natural density zero, and more precisely its counting function is asymptotic to sqrt(2N). Approach: invert the closed form A(k+1)+A(k) = k^2 + 2 to count solutions below N, then bound the counting function between consecutive squares. This formalizes the brief's 'complement has density zero' claim for the correctly-identified sequence.",
    "tags": ["DensityZero", "AdditiveNumberTheory"]
  },
  {
    "title": "Golden-ratio evasion as a spectral gap",
    "description": "The anti-Fibonacci ratio stays bounded away from the golden ratio φ ≈ 1.618 for all n ≥ 2 (it is at most 2 and decreases to 1). Conjecture: define the 'golden defect' of a positive sequence as the infimum over n of |B(n+1)/B(n) - φ|; then every sequence of polynomial growth has strictly positive golden defect, whereas every sequence obeying a Fibonacci-type recurrence has golden defect zero. Approach: for polynomial sequences use the ratio → 1 result to get a uniform gap below φ; for recurrence sequences use convergence to φ. This reframes 'avoiding the golden ratio' as a quantitative gap functional separating two universality classes of sequences.",
    "tags": ["GoldenRatio", "SpectralGap"]
  }
]

